# NestJS Rules

Follow the canonical backend feature structure in `.ai/STRUCTURE.md`.

Each feature module includes its required module, controller, service, DTO, entity, and response DTO structure.

- Controllers own HTTP routes, request DTOs, guards, endpoint authorization, and HTTP response boundaries. Keep them thin.
- Apply authorization through existing guards and decorators on controllers or endpoints. Do not duplicate it in services without a real non-HTTP use case.
- Services own linear business flows: load data, validate realistic business conditions, calculate, persist, and return the response DTO.
- Inject and use the TypeORM repository directly in the feature service.
- Do not add repository layers, use cases, handlers, managers, mappers, factories, or generic service abstractions.
- Keep all feature request and response DTO classes in the feature's single DTO file.
- Never return a TypeORM entity directly from an API endpoint. Map it explicitly to the response DTO at the local service or controller boundary.
- Validate request shape in DTOs and business rules in services. Add only realistic validation.
- Use built-in NestJS exceptions unless a custom exception has a concrete project-level benefit.
- Use the approved global exception filter and logger for consistent errors and meaningful failure logs. Do not add a logging library without approval.
- Treat schema changes as explicit feature work. Follow the existing migration convention and do not make unrelated schema changes.
